  "summary": "S96 terminal: 60 paid actions, 61 observations and 60 verified ROS arrivals; interrupted during goal 61 by chassis speed-feedback protection. CAN distance 11.0818355615 m over 652.883974261 s. Return was incomplete; zero motion was verified after stop. The offending retained CAN frame reports 0.154 m/s against the unchanged strict >0.15 m/s stop threshold. Native fault 8 was the external guard response to this speed fault, not recurrence of native lock fault 6. Laser observed union rose from 61.72 to 117.92 m\u00b2 (56.2 m\u00b2); fixed-support Q decreased by 0.000805635979. All three nonempty recorded plans had equal G/S scores. These results retain the failure and do not establish semantic benefit. The separate 0.05 m/s cap and short trial were not part of this S96 trial; later evidence follows below.\n\nThe separately deployed nso_dwa_linear_consistency_005_v1 profile clips positive NSO/joint adapter commands from 0.10 to 0.05 m/s to match declared DWA max_vel_x/max_vel_trans. It adds bounded fault evidence. All CAN stop thresholds and research policy remain unchanged. Candidate source and deployment hashes are retained separately from frozen S96 source. The candidate test manifest records 43 passing tests and the full-suite pre-existing lifecycle harness errors, with baseline evidence retained. Historical candidate documents describe isolation before deployment; the later deployment receipt records actual production activation. The separate short12 trial was initially pending; its final result is recorded below.\n\nThe separate short12 trial subsequently completed: 12 paid actions, 13 accepted observations and 12 verified ROS arrivals, with 0.9988825539 m travel over approximately 152.7668 s. It returned to anchor and stopped. The independent review records 55 passing tests and its excluded baseline lifecycle caveat. This dependent short validation does not establish general loaded stability or resolve the original S96 failure causally. A further 40-action validation was launched under its own frozen configuration and retained navigation map; its failure is reported below. The receive-only passive CAN maxima recorder started after that mission initialization: its results cover only its recorded start/end interval. No full-mission command bound may be inferred from that partial interval. S40 terminal evidence is now available below.\n\nThe new-cap S40 subsequently failed after 9 paid actions, 10 observations and 9 verified arrivals (1.4837 m, 136.336 s), with incomplete return. Retained commands stayed within 0.05 m/s during the passive recorder interval while feedback peaked at 0.159 m/s. Short12 success therefore did not establish that the cap resolved the fault. Both failures are preserved.\n\nThe user subsequently explicitly approved measured_020_v1: the linear feedback stop limit changes from 0.15 to 0.20 m/s; command cap remains 0.05 m/s, and other stops and research policy remain unchanged. The isolated proposal manifest is a historical snapshot; the later deployment receipt records approval and activation. A predeclared within-scene C/G40 comparison is RUNNING at this publication snapshot. Only its protocol, common frame and C configuration are included; no completed comparison outcome or benefit is asserted. Paper and thesis updates predate this new feedback-limit comparison and preserve the completed prior evidence."
}
